// What is the probability of occurence of the (S,S,C) state, i.e., the sunny weather on two days followed by a cloudy, in future?
P=? [ F x=1 ]

// What is the probability of occurence of the (S,S,S) state, i.e., sunny state if yesterday and day before yesterday was sunny, in future?
P=? [ F x=0 ]

// What is the probability of occurence of the (C,C,C) state, i.e., cloudy state if yesterday and the day before yesterday was cloudy, in future?
P=? [ F x=8 ]

// What is the probability of occurence of the (C,C,R) state, i.e., the cloudy weather on two days followed by a rainy, in future?
P=? [ F x=9 ]

// What is the probability of occurence of the (R,R,R) state, i.e., the probability of occurrence of three consecutive rainy days (today is also rainy, where
yesterday and the day before yesterday were rainy)?
P=? [ F x=12 ]

// What is the probability of occurence of the (R,R,C) state, i.e., a rainy weather on two consecutive days followed by a cloudy weather, in future?
P=? [ F x=13 ]

// Probability of (S,S,S) weather state at the first day is equal to 1.
P>=1 [ F<=1 x=0 ]

// What is the probability of (C,C,C) weather state within the first five days?
P=? [ F<=5 x=8 ]

// The probability of occurrence of the state (S,C,R) within 7 days, given the initial state, is always less than 0.1.
P < 0.1 [ F<=7 x=4 ]

// What is the probability of (S,S,S) weather state initially?
P=? [ F<1 x=0 ]

// What is the probability of occurrence of (S,S,S) weather state within four days?
P=? [ F<=4 x=0 ]

// What is the probability of occurrence of (S,S,S) weather state within seven days?
P=? [ F<=7 x=0 ]

// What is the probability of (S,S,C) weather within seven days?
P=? [ F<=7 x=1 ]

// What is the probability of (S,S,C) weather state with an initial state as (S,S,S)?
P=? [ F<1 x=1 ]

// What is the probability of (R,R,C) weather within seven days?
P=? [ F<=7 x=13 ]

// Probability of occurrence of the cloudy state on three consecutive days, i.e., (C,C,C) during the seond week is greater than 0.09.
P>0.09 [ F [8,14] x=8 ]

// What is the probability of occurrence of the rainy state on three consecutive days, i.e., (R,R,R) during the second week?
P=? [ F [8,14] x=12 ]



// // // // //

// We formally verify the probability of observing extended rainfall patterns by analyzing the likelihood of persistent rain-dominant states over multiple consecutive time steps within a bounded horizon of 30 days. This property is expressed using PCTL, where the next operator (X) refers to the immediate successor state. The use of successive X operators enables the characterization of consecutive occurrences of rainfall, thereby capturing sustained precipitation events.
P=? [ F<=30 ("heavy_rain" & X "heavy_rain" & X X "heavy_rain") ]


// We verify the probability of occurrence of rainfall within a bounded time horizon using the eventually operator (F), which specifies that a given condition will hold at some point in the future. This allows us to quantify the likelihood of precipitation occurring within the next few days.
P=? [ F<=7 "rain" ]


// We formally analyze the probability of transitioning from dry weather conditions to rainfall within a specified time interval using the until operator (U). This operator captures the notion that dry conditions persist continuously until rainfall occurs, thereby modeling the onset of precipitation following a dry spell.
P=? [ "dry" U<=7 "rain" ]


// We verify the probability of maintaining rain-free conditions over a specified time horizon using the globally operator (G), which ensures that a given condition holds at all time steps within the bounded interval. This property captures prolonged dry periods that are relevant for agricultural and environmental planning.
P=? [ G<=7 "dry" ]


// We verify the likelihood of precipitation following sustained cloudy conditions by analyzing transitions from fully cloudy states to rain-dominant states within a short-term horizon. This captures a common meteorological progression from cloud accumulation to rainfall.
P=? [ (x=8) => F<=3 "rain" ]


// We analyze the persistence of rainfall by verifying the probability of reaching and maintaining fully rainy states within a bounded time horizon, thereby providing insight into the duration and stability of precipitation events.
P=? [ F<=5 "RRR" ]


// We verify the probability of recovery from sustained rainfall to clear weather within a bounded time interval, capturing the transition from adverse to favorable weather conditions.
P=? [ (x=12) => F<=5 "SSS" ]

// We analyze the stability of clear weather conditions by verifying that sunny states persist throughout a specified time horizon, indicating periods of atmospheric stability.
P=? [ G<=5 "SSS" ]

// We evaluate the probability of oscillatory weather behavior characterized by frequent transitions between sunny and cloudy conditions, reflecting unstable atmospheric dynamics.
P=? [ F<=10 (x=2 | x=6) ]






























